<style>
  /* Dark-first: the subject is a lit board in a dark metal frame, so :root
     carries the dark palette and light is the deliberate second theme. The
     board plate itself stays dark in both -- it is a physical instrument, and
     the marble colours are calibrated against it. */
  :root {
    --ink:        #12130F;
    --panel:      #1C1E18;
    --raised:     #262922;
    --line:       #34382C;
    --text:       #E6E3D3;
    --muted:      #9B9A85;
    --faint:      #6E6E5D;
    --brass:      #C9A227;
    --brass-dim:  #8A7118;
    --verdigris:  #5FA98F;
    --mars:       #C4553B;
    --shadow:     rgba(0, 0, 0, .55);

    /* The board is a lit instrument in a dark frame, so its own colours are
       fixed in both themes -- the marble colours are calibrated against them,
       and a board that inverted with the page would need a second palette. */
    --plate:      #14160F;
    --cell:       #26291F;
    --cell-edge:  #383C2E;
    --plate-edge: #43482F;

    --display: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Palatino, "Book Antiqua", Georgia, serif;
    --body: ui-sans-serif, "Segoe UI", system-ui, -apple-system, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    --mono: "Cascadia Mono", "Cascadia Code", Consolas, ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, monospace;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
    :root:not([data-theme="dark"]) {
      --ink:       #EDE9DC;
      --panel:     #F6F3E9;
      --raised:    #FFFDF5;
      --line:      #D3CCB8;
      --text:      #23241C;
      --muted:     #5F5F4E;
      --faint:     #86846F;
      --brass:     #8A6D0B;
      --brass-dim: #A98C22;
      --verdigris: #2F7D62;
      --mars:      #A63A22;
      --shadow:    rgba(60, 55, 35, .18);
    }
  }
  :root[data-theme="light"] {
    --ink:       #EDE9DC;
    --panel:     #F6F3E9;
    --raised:    #FFFDF5;
    --line:      #D3CCB8;
    --text:      #23241C;
    --muted:     #5F5F4E;
    --faint:     #86846F;
    --brass:     #8A6D0B;
    --brass-dim: #A98C22;
    --verdigris: #2F7D62;
    --mars:      #A63A22;
    --shadow:    rgba(60, 55, 35, .18);
  }

  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--body);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
  }
  .wrap { max-width: 1120px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 40px 22px 80px; }

  /* ---- masthead ---- */
  header { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 30px; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; letter-spacing: .18em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--brass);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -.015em;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5.5vw, 56px); line-height: 1.04; margin: 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .standfirst {
    font-family: var(--display); font-size: clamp(17px, 2.2vw, 21px);
    color: var(--muted); max-width: 62ch; margin: 0; line-height: 1.45;
  }

  /* ---- the workbench ---- */
  .bench { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 300px; gap: 20px; align-items: start; }
  @media (max-width: 880px) { .bench { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); } }

  .plate {
    background: var(--plate);
    border: 1px solid var(--plate-edge);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 14px;
    box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, .04), 0 12px 34px -18px var(--shadow);
  }
  .plate svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }

  aside { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; }
  .card {
    background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 4px; padding: 13px 14px;
  }
  .card h3 {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .16em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--faint);
    margin: 0 0 9px; font-weight: 600;
  }
  .readout {
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 10px;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 13px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .readout b { color: var(--brass); font-weight: 600; }
  #status { font-size: 13.5px; min-height: 3.2em; margin-top: 8px; color: var(--muted); }
  #status.good { color: var(--verdigris); }
  #status.bad { color: var(--mars); }

  .buttons { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 7px; }
  button {
    font: inherit; font-size: 13px; padding: 8px 6px; cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--text); background: var(--raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 3px;
    transition: border-color .12s, background .12s;
  }
  button:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: var(--brass); background: var(--panel); }
  button:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--brass); outline-offset: 2px; }
  button:disabled { opacity: .4; cursor: default; }
  button.key { border-color: var(--brass-dim); color: var(--brass); }
  .buttons .wide { grid-column: 1 / -1; }

  #tally { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(7, 1fr); gap: 6px 4px; }
  .chip { text-align: center; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10px; color: var(--faint); }
  .chip.gone { opacity: .28; }
  #plan {
    max-height: 190px; overflow-y: auto; font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.7; scrollbar-width: thin;
  }
  #plan div { padding: 0 4px; border-radius: 2px; white-space: pre; }
  #plan div.done { opacity: .3; }
  #plan div.now { background: var(--raised); color: var(--brass); }

  #drop {
    border: 1px dashed var(--line); border-radius: 4px; padding: 13px 14px;
    background: var(--panel); transition: border-color .12s;
  }
  #drop.hot { border-color: var(--brass); }
  #drop p { margin: 0 0 9px; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); }
  kbd {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; padding: 1px 5px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 3px; background: var(--raised);
  }

  /* ---- board pieces ---- */
  .hex { fill: var(--cell); stroke: var(--cell-edge); stroke-width: 1; }
  .marble { transition: opacity .14s; }
  .marble.locked { opacity: .3; }
  .cell.live { cursor: pointer; }
  .ring { fill: none; }
  .ring.pick { stroke: var(--brass); stroke-width: 3; }
  .ring.mate { stroke: var(--verdigris); stroke-width: 2.5; stroke-dasharray: 4 4; }
  .ring.hint { stroke: #6FC6E8; stroke-width: 3.5; }
  .ring.doubt { stroke: var(--mars); stroke-width: 2.5; stroke-dasharray: 3 3; }
  @keyframes breathe { 0%, 100% { opacity: 1 } 50% { opacity: .25 } }
  .ring.mate, .ring.hint { animation: breathe 1.15s ease-in-out infinite; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .ring.mate, .ring.hint { animation: none; }
    * { transition: none !important; }
  }

  /* ---- the write-up ---- */
  article { max-width: 66ch; margin: 62px auto 0; }
  article section { margin-bottom: 46px; }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--display); font-size: 27px; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 0 0 4px; text-wrap: balance;
  }
  h2 + .kicker {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: .14em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--brass); margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  h3.sub {
    font-family: var(--body); font-size: 15.5px; font-weight: 600;
    margin: 26px 0 6px; color: var(--text);
  }
  article p { margin: 0 0 14px; }
  article ul { margin: 0 0 14px; padding-left: 20px; }
  article li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  article code {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .87em;
    background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 3px; padding: 1px 5px;
  }
  .rule {
    border: 0; height: 1px; background: var(--line);
    margin: 0 0 46px; position: relative; overflow: visible;
  }
  .rule::after {
    content: "☉"; position: absolute; left: 50%; top: -.72em;
    transform: translateX(-50%); background: var(--ink);
    padding: 0 12px; color: var(--brass); font-size: 15px;
  }
  figure { margin: 0 0 18px; }
  figure pre {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.55;
    background: var(--panel); color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 14px; overflow-x: auto; margin: 0;
  }
  figcaption { font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--faint); margin-top: 7px; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 13.5px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
  th, td {
    text-align: left; padding: 7px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  th {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--faint); font-weight: 600;
  }
  td.num { text-align: right; font-family: var(--mono); }
  .legend { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(128px, 1fr)); gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 16px; }
  .legend div { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; font-size: 13px; }
  .legend span { color: var(--muted); }
  footer {
    max-width: 66ch; margin: 0 auto; padding-top: 26px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--faint); font-size: 13px;
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 18px; flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  footer a { color: var(--brass); text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid transparent; }
  footer a:hover, footer a:focus-visible { border-bottom-color: var(--brass); }
</style>

<div class="wrap">
  <header>
    <div class="eyebrow">Opus Magnum · Sigmar's Garden</div>
    <h1>A solver that reads the board off your screen and plays it</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Sigmar's Garden is the marble puzzle tucked inside Opus Magnum. This page is
      the whole thing working in your browser: play it, let it solve it, or paste a
      screenshot of the real game and watch it read all 55 marbles back.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="bench">
    <div class="plate"><svg id="board" viewBox="0 0 700 660" aria-label="Sigmar's Garden board"></svg></div>

    <aside>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="readout"><span>Marbles left</span> <b id="left">55</b></div>
        <div class="readout"><span>Moves played</span> <b id="played">0</b></div>
        <div id="status">Click a lit marble, then a matching one. Faded marbles are blocked.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Play</h3>
        <div class="buttons">
          <button id="new" class="key">New board</button>
          <button id="reset">Reset</button>
          <button id="undo">Undo</button>
          <button id="hint">Hint</button>
          <button id="check" class="wide">Can this still be won?</button>
          <button id="autoplay" class="key wide">Solve it for me</button>
        </div>
        <div id="plan" style="margin-top:10px"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="card" id="drop">
        <h3>Read a screenshot</h3>
        <p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> to paste a screenshot of the game, or drop an image here.</p>
        <div class="buttons"><button id="pick" class="wide">Choose an image…</button></div>
        <input type="file" id="file" accept="image/*" hidden>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Marbles remaining</h3>
        <div id="tally"></div>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <article>
    <hr class="rule">

    <section>
      <h2>How the game works</h2>
      <p class="kicker">Fifty-five marbles, twenty-eight moves</p>
      <p>
        Marbles sit on a hexagonal board of 91 cells. You clear them in matching
        pairs, and you win by clearing the lot. Two rules do all the work.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">A marble is only free if three of its six neighbours are empty and touching</h3>
      <p>
        Not any three — three <em>in a row</em> around it. Two gaps on opposite
        sides do not count. Space off the edge of the board counts as empty, which
        is why the rim clears first and the middle is the fight.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">What matches what</h3>
      <div class="legend" id="legend"></div>
      <ul>
        <li>The four elements — fire, water, earth, air — pair with their own kind.</li>
        <li>Salt pairs with any element, or with another salt. It is the flexible one, and only four exist.</li>
        <li>Vitae and mors pair only with each other.</li>
        <li>The five metals go in order of transmutation — lead, tin, iron, copper, silver — each with a quicksilver. Tin cannot go while lead is still down.</li>
        <li>Gold clears alone, once it is the last metal standing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A full deal is always the same multiset: eight of each element, four salt,
        four vitae, four mors, five quicksilver, and one of each metal. That adds up
        to 55 marbles and exactly 28 moves — 27 pairs and the gold. The count turns
        out to matter enormously later.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule">

    <section>
      <h2>Solving it</h2>
      <p class="kicker">A search that fits in one integer</p>
      <p>
        A marble never moves, so its position tells you its type. That means the
        whole state of a game is just <em>which cells are still occupied</em> — 91
        bits, one integer. The solver is a depth-first search over those integers
        that remembers every state it has proven unwinnable, so a dead end reached
        by a different order of moves is recognised instead of re-explored.
      </p>
      <p>
        Freedom is a lookup, not a calculation. Each cell's six neighbours are
        stored in cyclic order, so "three contiguous empty spaces" is a window over
        a 64-entry table. Directions that run off the board point at a bit that is
        never set, so edge cells count their missing sides as empty for free.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">Two facts about the game shape the search</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Removing marbles never blocks anything.</strong> Freedom only ever
        increases. So the difficulty is not the order of the moves, it is the
        <em>pairing</em>: spend a salt on an element that still had a partner of its
        own kind, and you can orphan a marble twenty moves later. Salt is therefore
        played last, and the search carries a parity check — each element left in an
        odd number must consume a salt, and the leftover salt has to pair with
        itself. Positions failing that are dead before the search notices.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Metals are forced.</strong> Only the lowest metal on the board can
        go, and only against quicksilver, so those moves have no alternative pairing
        and cost nothing to take early.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">Restarts, and why the jitter has to be big</h3>
      <p>
        Solving times are heavy-tailed. The median board falls in 29 states; every
        so often one choice near the root buries the search in a barren subtree. So
        the search runs in rounds of doubling budgets, re-shuffling its tie-breaks
        each round, and <em>keeps</em> the set of dead states across rounds —
        deadness does not depend on how a state was reached, so a restart never
        repeats prior work, it only re-picks its gambles.
      </p>
      <p>
        The size of the shuffle turned out to be the whole trick. With a small
        jitter, restarts only reshuffled marbles <em>within</em> a class of move,
        kept re-walking the same doomed opening, and the two worst boards in 300
        needed over 1.5 million states. Making the jitter large enough to reconsider
        which <em>kind</em> of move to open with brought both under 140,000.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">Two good ideas that measurement threw out</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Scoring moves by how many marbles they actually unlock searched fewer states but cost more wall-clock than it saved.</li>
        <li>A fixpoint "could this marble ever be freed?" prune never fired once across 600 boards — these boards die on pairing, not geometry — and cost two to three times as much.</li>
      </ul>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Boards</th><th>Result</th><th class="num">Median</th><th class="num">Worst</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>300 generated solvable</td><td>300 solved</td><td class="num">0.6 ms</td><td class="num">11.9 s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>300 dense random deals</td><td>68 solved, 232 <em>proven</em> unsolvable</td><td class="num">23 ms</td><td class="num">2.8 s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Test boards are built backwards — pairs of a solution placed onto an empty
        board in reverse, each required to land free — so every generated board
        comes with a guaranteed solution, and the solver is checked against a real
        oracle rather than its own output.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule">

    <section>
      <h2>Reading the board off a screenshot</h2>
      <p class="kicker">Finding a hexagon, then fifty-five glyphs</p>
      <p>
        Transcribing 55 marbles by hand is miserable, so the next step was to read
        them from a picture. Two problems: find the board, then name every cell.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">Finding it</h3>
      <p>
        The playing surface is far brighter than the metal around it, so a
        brightness split isolates it — chosen per image, because a fixed threshold
        read the reference perfectly and then lost 60 cells on a copy 20% brighter.
        The board is a hexagon whose extent in a few directions pins down its centre
        and scale.
      </p>
      <p>
        On a whole desktop the board is <em>not</em> the biggest bright thing — a
        bright wallpaper beats it easily — so candidates are ranked by shape rather
        than size. A board's width and height imply the same cell size and it fills
        the area that size predicts; the wallpaper scored 37% skew and 0.55 fill
        against the board's 3% and 1.06.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">Naming the cells</h3>
      <p>
        The game draws blocked marbles washed out and free ones vivid, so the
        features have to ignore that entirely. Each cell is high-pass filtered — the
        tile minus a blurred copy of itself — which throws away the marble's shading
        and the cell's bevel and leaves the engraved glyph, then normalised so faint
        and bold match. Colour enters only as a <em>direction</em>: the disc's colour
        minus the cell background, scaled to unit length, because fading a marble
        toward the beige changes that vector's length but not its heading.
      </p>
      <p>Three things were found by measuring rather than assuming:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Plain contrast-stretched brightness gets 96.5% of cells, and its mistakes
          are exactly fire↔earth and vitae↔salt — pairs the eye separates instantly.
          The marble's radial shading dominates the tile and the glyph is a small
          part of the signal. High-pass filtering takes it to 100%.
        </li>
        <li>
          "Empty" has to be a template class, not a brightness threshold. Six
          blocked marbles — faint vitae, salt, quicksilver — sit <em>below</em> the
          brightest empty cell, so no threshold separates them. Their glyphs are
          unmistakable.
        </li>
        <li>
          Templates are stored at several small offsets each. Detection lands within
          a pixel or two but not exactly, and matching a glyph against a template
          shifted 2px loses the outer ring of cells.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The reader refuses rather than guessing when the board is too small to
        resolve, cut off by the crop, or not a board. Whatever slips past that is
        caught by the marble counts, because a misread almost always shows up as
        some count differing from a standard deal.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">The constraint that fixed the last bug</h3>
      <p>
        A long automated run once died after one win: lead read as mors — both dark
        marbles, and lead has the fewest examples because it appears once per board.
        The fix was to stop reading a fresh deal cell by cell. The counts are known
        exactly, so the board is solved as an assignment against them: the cell that
        <em>has</em> to be the lead is the one that fits it least badly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Testing that fix caught something worse. Imposing the counts always succeeds
        — on a board still animating in, it cheerfully invented 55 marbles from an
        empty table, and because the counts then matched by construction, the check
        that was supposed to validate the deal had been destroyed. A visible bug had
        been replaced with a silent one. So the constrained result is only accepted
        when the plain read already saw a full board and needed at most a couple of
        cells overruled.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule">

    <section>
      <h2>Playing the real game</h2>
      <p class="kicker">Screenshot, solve, click, verify</p>
      <p>
        The last piece drives the actual game: screenshot the monitor, find the
        board among everything else on screen, read it, solve it, and click the
        pairs. It talks to Windows directly rather than through an automation
        library, because the usual one scales its coordinates by the display's DPI
        setting and so misses every click on a 4K screen.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">It checks its own work</h3>
      <p>
        The loop is deliberately not "solve once, then fire 56 clicks and hope".
        After every pair it re-reads those two cells and confirms they actually went
        empty. A click that misses leaves the plan and the board disagreeing, and
        every later click then lands on the wrong marble — so it retries, more
        slowly, and then gives up on that board rather than clicking on blind.
      </p>
      <p>
        Failures say <em>which</em> thing went wrong, because the fixes differ:
        "they read as Fire and Water, which do not match — misread" versus "the pair
        still matches, so the click did not register". It also watches the game's own
        wins counter after each board. It never reads the number, only sees the box
        change, which is enough to confirm the game agreed — and that caught a real
        bug immediately, when a board left already-cleared from an earlier run
        "solved in 0 moves" and was scored as a win while the counter sat still.
      </p>
      <h3 class="sub">Making it quick</h3>
      <p>
        The first version took 1.37 seconds a move, nearly 40 seconds a board. Almost
        none of it was the clicking:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Screen capture cost 110ms a go, whatever the size.</strong> The
          imaging library rebuilds its device contexts on every call, so grabbing
          forty pixels cost the same as grabbing a 4K screen. Holding them open
          across calls takes the same grab to 6ms, pixel for pixel identical.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Verifying a move re-read all 91 cells</strong> to look at two.
          Reading just those two costs 27ms against 370.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A fixed 450ms sleep waited for the clearing animation.</strong>
          Polling instead finishes as soon as the pair actually goes.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That is 227ms a move, about six seconds a board. What is left is the game's
        own animations: dealing takes 4.3 seconds and nothing can hurry it, so the
        loop waits by watching the board stop changing — an idle board measures a
        frame-to-frame difference of exactly zero, which makes "still" unambiguous.
        It waits for the animation to <em>start</em> first, because the deal does not
        begin until 400ms after the click and the board is otherwise trivially still
        the moment it is asked.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <pre>64/64 wins over 65 boards, 1 abandoned
1,792 moves played, 1 click missed
7 of 65 deals needed the known counts to settle a cell</pre>
        <figcaption>
          One unattended run. Every win confirmed by the game's own counter. The
          seven corrected deals are each a board that, before that fix, would have
          ended the run.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule">

    <section>
      <h2>What is running where</h2>
      <p class="kicker">On this page, and in the repository</p>
      <p>
        Everything above this line runs in your browser — the rules, the search, and
        the screenshot reader are ported from the Python, using the same templates
        cut from two real screenshots. The solver here matches the original state
        for state: the reference board solves in 83.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Python project adds what a browser cannot do: driving the real game. It
        ships a command line tool, a local web front end for checking the rules by
        hand, and the autoplay loop, with 113 tests covering the geometry, every
        matching rule, the search, the reader and the click layer. The autoplay tests
        stub out the screen and the mouse entirely — nothing in the suite moves a
        real pointer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer>
      <span>
        Opus Magnum is by Zachtronics. The puzzle, its artwork and its
        alchemical glyphs are theirs; this is a solver for it.
      </span>{{HOME_LINK}}
    </footer>
  </article>
</div>

